        { "text": "The third is what a correction is worth when the company finds it itself. OpenAI's own revision log caught the sign error and withdrew the three papers. Anthropic found its own cases and wrote them up. In both, the publisher is also the auditor. The outside parties who did weigh in were not satisfied: a working group of the Association for Human Mathematics urged mathematicians to stop working with OpenAI, writing that releasing over 700 files at once is not a demonstration of scholarship but a demonstration of power. Meanwhile the White House told Axios that notification is not optional, while Axios reports the same statement named no penalties, and the European Commission's panel of 60 experts put questions to the companies whose models were involved without naming one." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "unknowns",
      "lines": [
        { "text": "Now the part I think matters most, which is what we still don't know. These are the week's open questions as the written edition carries them, and each one has a concrete test attached that would settle it." },
        { "text": "Will any regulator or court assign liability for an agent incident, now that two labs have reported their models acting on real systems? Nobody has yet. The White House requirement rests on one statement to one outlet with no instrument behind it, and the Commission's questions have not been published." },
        { "text": "Can the AI attribution in this month's intrusions be verified by anyone other than the vendor that made it? Every figure in both campaigns comes from a security company reading the attacker's own exposed files. No government body, no CERT and no second vendor has published comparable counts, and with ARTEX closed source, outside researchers can't examine the tool either." },
        { "text": "Which two frontier models met the objective in all eight of Booz Allen's attacks, and will any vendor confirm the result? Until someone answers that, the most serious capability claim of the week cannot be tested against any company's safeguards." },
        { "text": "And a straight factual conflict. How many people were killed in the strike Palantir's chief technology officer was asked about, and what does the final report say caused it? Margaret Brennan put the Minab strike at at least 123 schoolchildren. A separate report describes a US strike on an Iranian school killing more than 160 people and hedges the AI link as something that may have played a role. No source this week says those are the same event, Shyam Sankar said the Bloomberg reporting is wrong and pointed to human error, and Brennan said 120 House Democrats have written to the Secretary about it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "calendar",
      "lines": [
        { "text": "Two dates in the week ahead. Today, October 12th, NATO's Supreme Allied Commander Europe is expected to present the concept being called NATO's Theory of Victory in Warsaw, and to elaborate on it at the Warsaw Security Forum. The unclassified version reporters have seen describes a plan built on low-cost drones and AI-enabled targeting, and the document itself has not been published. Then on October 19th, the heads of five major South Korean commercial banks are summoned to a parliamentary audit to answer questions about the cybersecurity lapses behind the breaches we just went through." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "close",
      "lines": [
        { "text": "The full review is on the site, with a link behind every claim in it, including the ones I attributed out loud here. If a number in this episode mattered to you, go and read where it came from, and read the caveat sitting next to it, because this week most of the caveats are the story. The daily is back tomorrow morning with Maya and Alex. I'm Mike Shoss. Thanks for listening." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

