        { "text": "The second record is worse, and it came out of one exposed folder. SOCRadar says a Russian-speaking access broker it calls CyberXero was running up to 51 specialised Claude Code agents on one workstation, in roles with names like exploit-chainer and scada-attacker. Confirmed exfiltration covers four Ukrainian organisations and more than 628,000 records tied to Ukrainian individuals, the worst being a district heating provider in Kharkiv where an extractor pulled 564,073 subscriber records with home addresses and account numbers. The same logs show the model refusing: across four attempts it declined to install a backdoor, disable a firewall, move laterally and deploy a webshell." },
        { "text": "The third security record is a lab test, and it's the one I'd read twice. Booz Allen Hamilton ran eight scenarios against real industrial control equipment and told The Register the models achieved the objectives across all eight, turning digital access into physical actions. In one case they found and moved a robotic arm in minutes. In another they went from a perimeter compromise to actions inside the control network in just over 16 minutes. One gateway exposed live pre-authentication connections to 14 other devices. Booz Allen declined to say which two models it tested, so the most alarming result of the week is one nobody can check against a vendor, and the agents still needed human approval before anything physical." },
